personal (nasty remarks) and memory for numbers (attention/accuracy). Could be serious.\n\nC: makes correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. Strengths: decision quality, stress resilience, technical competence. Concerns: communication, egocentric, lack of development.\n\nD: responds to unexpected events adequately, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, hot temper. Strengths: problem solving, responsibility, concentration. Concerns: attitude (know-it-all, hot temper) may affect crew cooperation.\n\nWe need to pick best overall fit. Likely B has strong reliability and stress handling but interpersonal issues. C has good decision and calm but communication issues. D has responsibility but hot temper. A has technical but attitude.\n\nWhich candidate balances? Perhaps Candidate B's conscientiousness and stress handling are crucial, but nasty remarks could be severe for crew cooperation. Might be mitigated? But we need overall safety-critical. Communication and teamwork are essential. C's communication difficulty is a big red flag. D's hot temper also problematic. A's not open to new ideas also problematic.\n\nThus B might be best despite remarks, but we need to consider memory for numbers (attention). Could be mitigated with training. I'd argue B is strongest overall.\n\nThus my recommendation: Candidate B.\n\nWe need to reference existing discussion (none). We can say \"Given we are starting, I propose Candidate B based on X and Y\". Provide evidence.\n\nLet's craft."}
